The first stage should run `mvn clean verify` rather than just `mvn package`. The difference matters: **verify** executes integration tests and static analysis plugins bound to the `verify` phase, while `package` stops after unit tests and JAR creation. Skipping verify means Checkstyle violations and integration failures slip through CI silently.

The pipeline declares the JDK and Maven versions explicitly through the `tools` block so builds are reproducible across agents.

```groovy
pipeline {
    agent any
    tools {
        maven 'Maven-3.9'
        jdk 'JDK-17'
    }
    stages {
        stage('Build & Test') {
            steps { sh 'mvn clean verify' }
            post {
                always { junit 'target/surefire-reports/**/*.xml' }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The `post { always { junit } }` block is not optional polish — it publishes test results to the Jenkins dashboard even when the stage fails. Without it, a flaky test breaks the build but the failure details vanish from the Jenkins UI, making triage a guessing game. Test trend graphs also depend on consistent `junit` publishing across every build.

The Docker build stage tags the image with `${BUILD_NUMBER}` to create an **immutable link** between every running container and the exact Jenkins build that produced it. During an incident at 2 AM, this lets you map a pod's image tag back to a specific build, its test results, and the source commit.

Credentials enter the pipeline through `withCredentials`, which binds secrets to environment variables only within the block scope. Jenkins automatically masks these values in console output.

```groovy
stage('Docker Build & Push') {
    steps {
        withCredentials([usernamePassword(
            credentialsId: 'docker-registry-creds',
            usernameVariable: 'DOCKER_USER',
            passwordVariable: 'DOCKER_PASS'
        )]) {
            sh '''
                docker build -t registry.example.com/my-app:${BUILD_NUMBER} .
                echo $DOCKER_PASS | docker login registry.example.com -u $DOCKER_USER --password-stdin
                docker push registry.example.com/my-app:${BUILD_NUMBER}
            '''
        }
    }
}
```

The scoping is intentional: if you put credentials in the top-level `environment {}` block, every stage and every `sh` step can reference them. A rogue debug `echo` or a third-party plugin that logs environment variables would leak the password into the build console. The `withCredentials` block ensures the secret exists only where the Docker push actually needs it.

The `post` section at the pipeline level handles cleanup regardless of outcome. **`cleanWs()`** in the `always` block reclaims disk space on shared agents — without it, Maven caches and Docker layer exports silently fill disks until builds start failing for unrelated reasons. Failure notifications via Slack or email ensure the team learns when a deploy was never promoted.

```groovy
post {
    always { cleanWs() }
    failure {
        slackSend channel: '#builds',
            message: "FAILED: ${currentBuild.fullDisplayName}"
    }
}
```

Three production failure modes distinguish a senior pipeline design from a tutorial copy. First, **parallel stages** for independent work like unit tests and static analysis can halve pipeline duration, but require `stash`/`unstash` to pass artifacts between parallel branches. Second, missing `rollout status` in a Kubernetes deploy stage hides broken deployments — Jenkins marks the stage green because `kubectl set image` returned 0, even though pods are crash-looping. Third, workspace cleanup matters more than it sounds: a team running 200 builds per day on three shared agents without `cleanWs()` will exhaust disk within a week.

Mentioning these failure modes in an interview shows you have maintained CI systems under real load, not just configured a demo pipeline once.

The declarative format structures a pipeline into well-defined sections, each with a specific purpose. Jenkins parses this structure before allocating an executor, so a missing `stages` block or a typo in `agent` fails immediately with a clear error message.

A typical Jenkinsfile for a Spring Boot service follows this skeleton.

```groovy
pipeline {
    agent any
    environment {
        APP_NAME = 'my-service'
    }
    options {
        timeout(time: 30, unit: 'MINUTES')
        buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: '10'))
    }
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps { sh 'mvn clean package -DskipTests' }
        }
        stage('Test') {
            steps { sh 'mvn test' }
        }
        stage('Deploy') {
            when { branch 'main' }
            steps { sh './deploy.sh' }
        }
    }
    post {
        failure { mail to: 'team@co.com', subject: 'FAILED' }
        always  { cleanWs() }
    }
}
```

The **`when` directive** on the Deploy stage is critical for branching strategies — it ensures feature branch builds never accidentally deploy to production. The `options` section sets `timeout` to cap hung builds and `buildDiscarder` to prevent old build logs from filling the controller disk. Scripted pipelines lack this upfront validation entirely; a Groovy error surfaces only at runtime, potentially after minutes of compilation and testing have already consumed executor time.

The `withCredentials` step supports three credential types most Java teams encounter: username-password pairs for Docker registries, secret text for API tokens, and file credentials for kubeconfig or keystores. Each binding creates environment variables that exist only within the block.

The scoping pattern for a Docker push stage keeps registry access out of every other stage.

```groovy
stage('Push Image') {
    steps {
        withCredentials([usernamePassword(
            credentialsId: 'docker-hub-creds',
            usernameVariable: 'DOCKER_USER',
            passwordVariable: 'DOCKER_PASS'
        )]) {
            sh 'echo $DOCKER_PASS | docker login -u $DOCKER_USER --password-stdin'
            sh 'docker push registry.example.com/my-app:${BUILD_NUMBER}'
        }
    }
}
```

After the block closes, `DOCKER_USER` and `DOCKER_PASS` no longer exist in the shell environment. This limits the blast radius if a later stage runs a rogue script or a third-party plugin dumps environment variables. **Folder-scoped credentials** add another layer: a credential stored in a Jenkins folder is invisible to jobs outside that folder. This prevents a new project accidentally referencing production deployment keys belonging to another team.

The common interview mistake is placing credentials in the top-level `environment {}` block. That makes the secret available to every stage, every `sh` step, and every plugin invocation throughout the entire pipeline — exactly the blast radius you want to minimize.

The analysis stage must run after `mvn verify` — not after `mvn package` — because **JaCoCo's report goal** is bound to the `test` phase and Sonar needs the generated XML file. Running `sonar:sonar` without a coverage report means the quality gate evaluates code smells and duplications but has zero coverage data, which either silently passes or fails for the wrong reason.

The `withSonarQubeEnv` wrapper injects the server URL and authentication token from Jenkins Global Configuration, keeping credentials out of the Jenkinsfile.

```groovy
stage('SonarQube Analysis') {
    steps {
        withSonarQubeEnv('SonarQube') {
            sh 'mvn clean verify sonar:sonar -Dsonar.projectKey=my-app'
        }
    }
}
```

This single command compiles, runs tests, generates the JaCoCo XML report, and uploads everything to SonarQube. The key property is **`sonar.coverage.jacoco.xmlReportPaths`** — if it points to the wrong path, Sonar runs a full analysis with zero coverage. For multi-module Maven projects, run Sonar from the aggregator root and exclude sample or test-fixture modules with `-pl` so analysis time stays proportional to production code.

After analysis completes, SonarQube evaluates the quality gate asynchronously on its server. The `waitForQualityGate` step pauses the Jenkins pipeline until a webhook callback delivers the result. If the gate fails — coverage dropped below 80%, new critical bugs appeared, or duplication exceeded the threshold — the pipeline aborts before reaching deploy stages.

```groovy
stage('Quality Gate') {
    steps {
        timeout(time: 5, unit: 'MINUTES') {
            waitForQualityGate abortPipeline: true
        }
    }
}
```

The `timeout` wrapper is essential for operational resilience. If SonarQube is down or the webhook misconfigures, the pipeline would hang indefinitely without it. Five minutes is generous — most analyses return in under two minutes. The webhook must be configured on the SonarQube server side pointing to `http://jenkins-url/sonarqube-webhook/`, and this is the most commonly missed configuration step when teams first set up the integration.

**PR decoration** takes enforcement further by surfacing quality issues directly in GitHub pull request comments. Reviewers see new bugs and smells inline on the diff rather than navigating to a separate dashboard. This shifts quality feedback into the workflow where developers already work. Combined with branch-aware analysis that uses stricter gates on `main` and softer thresholds on feature branches, the integration balances velocity with quality enforcement across the development lifecycle.

The image build stage creates a Docker image from the Spring Boot JAR and pushes it to a container registry. Tagging with `${BUILD_NUMBER}` ensures every deployed container traces back to the Jenkins build that produced it.

The pipeline binds registry credentials through `withCredentials` so passwords never appear in the Jenkinsfile or build logs.

```groovy
environment {
    IMAGE_TAG = "registry.example.com/my-app:${BUILD_NUMBER}"
}
stage('Build & Push Image') {
    steps {
        withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: 'docker-creds',
            usernameVariable: 'USER', passwordVariable: 'PASS')]) {
            sh '''
                docker build -t ${IMAGE_TAG} .
                echo $PASS | docker login registry.example.com -u $USER --password-stdin
                docker push ${IMAGE_TAG}
            '''
        }
    }
}
```

The push must complete **before** the deploy stage runs. This sounds obvious, but in pipelines with parallel stages it is possible to trigger a Kubernetes rollout referencing a tag that has not yet arrived in the registry. The cluster pulls the image, gets a 404, and pods enter `ImagePullBackOff`. Structuring stages sequentially with explicit `needs` ordering prevents this race condition.

The deploy stage authenticates to the cluster using a kubeconfig stored as a Jenkins File credential, then updates the deployment's image tag. The critical addition is `kubectl rollout status`, which blocks until the new pods are ready — or fails if they crash-loop.

```groovy
stage('Deploy to Staging') {
    steps {
        withCredentials([file(credentialsId: 'kubeconfig-staging', variable: 'KUBECONFIG')]) {
            sh '''
                kubectl set image deployment/my-app app=${IMAGE_TAG}
                kubectl rollout status deployment/my-app --timeout=5m
            '''
        }
    }
}
```

Without `rollout status`, Jenkins marks the stage green as soon as `kubectl set image` returns — which is immediate. The actual rollout happens asynchronously. New pods could fail readiness probes, crash on startup due to a missing environment variable, or OOM-kill under load, and nobody knows until users report errors. The five-minute timeout catches these failures within the pipeline rather than relying on monitoring to detect them later.

The kubeconfig file credential rotates independently from the Jenkinsfile. **Separate credentials per environment** — staging and production each get their own kubeconfig ID — ensure a staging pipeline cannot accidentally target the production cluster.
